{
  "requirement": "A bundled skill MAY put material needed only in specialized situations in files it links to. Each such link MUST say when to read the file. Each bundled skill, recognized by its front matter, is a site:\n\n:::{sites} skills\n```yaml\nlanguage: markdown\npaths: [assets/skills]\nrule:\n  kind: minus_metadata\nmatch:\n  - \"---\\nname: example\\n---\\n\\n# Example\\n\"\nno_match:\n  - \"# Example\\n\"\n```\n:::",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement has two obligations: a skill MAY link to specialized material (nothing to check), and each such link MUST say when to read the file. Each bundled skill upholds the second on its own, so each site's own review covers it. Neither obligation is left over for code to carry out.\n\nSite query `skills`: running it selects the five `assets/skills/*/SKILL.md` roots: `habeas`, `habeas-requirements`, `habeas-evidence`, `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` and `habeas-refactor`. A text search for `^name: ` finds the same five skill files. The other matches are the three CI workflow YAML files, which are outside the markdown and `assets/skills` scope. The skill reference files (`references/*.md`) have no front matter, so the query does not select them. It misses no bundled skill and selects nothing outside scope. Its match snippet has front matter. Its no_match snippet is a heading with no front matter, which is nearby code the query must not select.\n\nThe supplementary test `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bundled_skills_say_when_to_read_each_reference_file]` is not needed for adequacy. It would also back up the obligation: it checks each link's wording, and it fails if any `references/*.md` pointer in a skill has no checked reading guidance.",
